WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:04.780
They're calling this an almost unfathomable
amount of rain

00:00:04.780 --> 00:00:06.360
hitting this part of Texas, guys.

00:00:06.360 --> 00:00:08.040
With natural disasters in the news,

00:00:08.040 --> 00:00:12.379
the country is being treated to a real-time
debate over the sins of so-called price gouging,

00:00:12.379 --> 00:00:15.290
If you're price gouging in our state, I'm
going to go after you.

00:00:15.290 --> 00:00:17.200
And I will shame you on national TV.

00:00:17.200 --> 00:00:20.440
Defenders of gouging, including many libertarians,

00:00:20.450 --> 00:00:23.900
stress that price hikes force customers to
prioritize their purchases

00:00:23.900 --> 00:00:27.380
while incentivizing suppliers to bring necessities
to market.

00:00:27.380 --> 00:00:29.900
Marking the price up, selling it high as hell.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:32.580
So if jugs of water are selling at $10 a gallon,

00:00:32.580 --> 00:00:35.160
businesses have a motivation to truck them
in.

00:00:35.160 --> 00:00:39.320
One of the advantages of letting prices rise
is it forces people to make the tough decisions.

00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:43.100
And at that price, no one’s going to use
that water to wash their cars.

00:00:43.100 --> 00:00:46.120
Critics, however, say that it’s immoral
to use the price mechanism

00:00:46.120 --> 00:00:48.440
to meter out essentials in a crisis.

00:00:48.440 --> 00:00:51.260
You know we're gonna now see people coming
in from other states,

00:00:51.260 --> 00:00:54.800
trying to sell generators for a thousand dollars
to take care of-

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:56.580
you know to hurt people in need.

00:00:56.580 --> 00:00:59.360
It's disgusting, if anybody price gouges

00:00:59.360 --> 00:01:01.930
this is not the time to take advantage of
our neighbors.

00:01:01.930 --> 00:01:04.760
Yet both sides ignore a more-important reality:

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:08.670
Local and national businesses routinely give
away goods and services

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.469
much more efficiently than public-sector responders
or charities can manage.

00:01:12.469 --> 00:01:14.289
We saw what was happening in Florida

00:01:14.289 --> 00:01:17.639
we thought what could we do to help our neighbors
in Florida as well.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.200
In the wake of Hurricane Harvey slamming Texas,

00:01:20.200 --> 00:01:23.180
businesses pledged over $72 million in aid,

00:01:23.180 --> 00:01:27.180
with over three dozen giving more than $1
million a piece.

00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:31.240
Airbnb used its home-sharing network to set
up places for people to stay,

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:36.420
and the crowd-sourced mapping app Waze helped
the displaced find shelters.

00:01:36.420 --> 00:01:39.140
Walmart, the left’s favorite corporate bogeyman,

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:43.240
pledged more $20 million and brought water
and food to the needy.

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:46.880
During Hurricane Katrina, the world’s largest
retailer trucked in relief

00:01:46.880 --> 00:01:49.280
long after FEMA convoys stopped running.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.200
We will also match those donations two to
one up to ten million dollars

00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:55.180
bringing the whole total to thirty million.

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:59.440
Private sector aid gets less press than empty
shelves and gouging accusations,

00:01:59.450 --> 00:02:01.729
but it also makes good business sense.

00:02:01.729 --> 00:02:04.840
In some cases, price hikes during a crisis
are appropriate,

00:02:04.840 --> 00:02:08.819
but most retailers know they’re better off
showing that they care about their customers

00:02:08.819 --> 00:02:12.099
and aren’t out to take advantage of a bad
situation.

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:15.340
Successful businesses safeguard their reputations.

00:02:15.340 --> 00:02:17.560
In The Wealth of Nations, Adam Smith wrote that he had

00:02:17.560 --> 00:02:22.040
“never known much good done by” people motivated by charitable aims,”

00:02:22.040 --> 00:02:26.430
but providing aid in disasters is every bit
as much a part of the free market

00:02:26.430 --> 00:02:29.240
as more obvious forms of profit seeking.

00:02:29.240 --> 00:02:33.700
If critics and defenders of gouging are really
interested in creating a better society,

00:02:33.700 --> 00:02:38.400
they’d do well to help make this part of
the Invisible Hand visible to all.

